FamilyTree.lineages([{"key":"Bundervoet","head":"renee","caption":"Father's line → Evergem farmers, then the Oostende docks. Documented to c.1560.","origin":"Probably a place-name: 'the ford by the bunder' — a stream-crossing (voorde, spelled -voet in Flemish dialect, as in Dievoet ← Dievoort) beside a field called a bunder, an old land measure of roughly one hectare. A locational byname that hardened into a surname; still concentrated in its Evergem/Ghent cradle, exactly where our Petrus Bundervoet (~1560) lived."},{"key":"De Keyser","head":"cosette","caption":"Cosette's father's line — Oostende (via Hamme); a wartime birth in London.","origin":"'The emperor' (De Keizer / Kaiser) — a nickname surname. Usually given to the champion of a shooting guild (its 'keizer,' who won the yearly contest three years running), to someone who played the emperor in a procession, or after a house named 'In den Keizer'."},{"key":"Thumas","head":"dorien","caption":"Dorien's father's line — Grez-Doiceau (Walloon Brabant) → Kraainem, 1872. Documented to 1656 (recent link assumed).","origin":"A Walloon dialect form of Thomas — a baptismal surname meaning 'descendant of a man named Thomas'. The first name Thomas comes from the Aramaic t'oma, 'twin' (Greek Didymos). Fitting for this Walloon-Brabant line."},{"key":"Janssens","head":"christiane","caption":"Dorien's mother's line — Brabant (Zaventem/Kraainem area). 20th-c. privacy wall.","origin":"A patronymic — 'son of Jan' (Jan = Johannes / John), the -s marking 'Jan's [son]'. John being the commonest medieval Christian name, Janssens is among the most frequent surnames in all of Belgium."}]);
