FamilyTree.person({"id":"francl_s","name":"Franciscus Leopoldus Smessaert","dates":"","confidence":"sup","surname":"Smessaert","family":"smessaert","branch":"Devriendt","line":"devriendt","spouses":[{"id":"morree","name":"Anna Francisca Morree"}],"source":"Marriage act — Franciscus Léopoldus Smessaert × Anna Francisca Morbeé, Brugge, 16 August 1851","note":"STRONG LEAD, NOT GRAFTED (July 2026). A Brugge marriage act of 16 August 1851 ([[S12]])\nrecords a **Franciscus Léopoldus Smessaert** marrying an **Anna Francisca Morbeé**, and\nnames both sets of parents: Paulus Smessaert x Isabella Claeys for him, Petrus Josephus\nMorbeé x Joanna De Clerck for her.\n\nThree things fit: the groom's triple name is distinctive and matches exactly, the bride's\nforenames match exactly, and a marriage in 1851 sits right for a son born at Oostende in\n1857. Brugge and Oostende are neighbours.\n\nOne thing does not: the bride is **Morbeé**, and this tree calls her **Morree**. That is a\ndisagreement, not a silence, and it is the only surname either of these two records has.\nEverything else here — both of them have no dates, no places and no source of their own —\ncomes from the wernero tree, so there is nothing independent to break the tie with. Under\nthe two-identifier rule this is a lead, and it stays one: no parents have been grafted.\n\nDECISIVE RECORD: [[joannesf_s]]'s Oostende birth act of 12 January 1857, which would give\nhis mother's surname as the registrar wrote it. If it says Morbeé, this act is ours and\nfour new ancestors come with it; if it says Morree, the couple above are somebody else's."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"georges1_t","name":"Georges Thumas","dates":"14 Mar 1744 Grez-Doiceau – 20 Nov 1808 Grez-Doiceau","confidence":"doc","surname":"Thumas","family":"thumas","born":"14 Mar 1744 Grez-Doiceau","order":"1744-03-14","died":"20 Nov 1808 Grez-Doiceau","occupation":"menuisier (schrijnwerker / carpenter)","branch":"Thumas","line":"thumas","father":"jeanlambert_t","mother":"leclercq_t","spouses":[{"id":"noel_t","name":"Marie Catherine Noël","detail":"Grez-Doiceau, 3 Jan 1771"}],"source":"België, Brabant, burgerlijke stand, 1582-1950; Death act nr. 58 — George Thumas, Grez-Doiceau, 20 November 1808 (read on AGATHA); Death act of George Thumas, Grez-Doiceau, 20 November 1808; m2155","note":"The first of FOUR consecutive carpenter (menuisier) generations of Thumas in Grez-Doiceau. Recorded as a godfather (1771) and marriage witness (1775).\n\nCORROBORATED (July 2026) by his own death act — Grez-Doiceau, 20 November 1808, the date\nthis record already held, to the day. The act names his father as Lambert Thumas, his\nmother as Marie Leclercq and his wife as Marie Catherine Noël, so it confirms three links\nat once: [[jeanlambert_t]], [[leclercq_t]] and the marriage to [[noel_t]], all of which\nstood on the m2155 tree alone until now.\n\nA NEAR-MISS WORTH KEEPING. Before the Thumas harvest was complete, the corpus offered a\ndifferent man for this record and scored him STRONG: *Martin Joseph* Thumas, who died at\nGrez-Doiceau on 15 February 1808 — same surname, same parish, same year, three agreeing\nidentifiers. He was rejected because the given names are different men and the days do\nnot match, and that rejection was right; the true act only appeared once more of the\nsurname had been harvested. The lesson is the one the charter states: surname plus place\nplus year is three agreements and still not an identification.\n\nACT READ (July 2026). His own death act — Grez-Doiceau, akte nr. 58, registered 21 November\n1808 and stating the death as the **20th** — has been read at the archive and saved\n([[george-thumas-1808-death-grez-doiceau]]). It confirms the death date exactly, gives his\ntrade as *menuisier*, the word this record already carried, and names his father, mother and\nwife: [[jeanlambert_t]], [[leclercq_t]] and [[noel_t]]. Three links off a member tree and\nonto a civil act.\n\n**A conflict, left open.** The act says he was **60**, which puts his birth around 1747–48;\nthis record says **14 March 1744**, which would make him 64. The birth date is unchanged,\nbecause an age in a death act is hearsay — reported here by a 32-year-old day-labourer — and\nroutinely a few years out, while a stated date is at least a claim about a particular record.\nNeither is documented. The Grez-Doiceau baptisms for 1744 and 1747–48 would settle it.\n\nTHE AGE CONFLICT IS NOT SETTLED BY FAMILYSEARCH — and the reason matters. Its index of this\nfamily gives his birth as **1748**, which looks at first like independent support for the\n1808 act's \"age 60\" against this record's 1744. It is not independent: FamilySearch is\nindexing *that same act*, and 1748 is simply 1808 minus 60. Two sources agreeing are not two\nsources when one is computed from the other.\n\nWhat does bear on it is the family's own shape. His parents married at Wavre on\n**23 February 1743**, and FamilySearch holds a sister, **Catherine Josephe Thumas**, born\n**1745** to the same Lambert Thumas × Marie Leclercq and buried at Grez-Doiceau on 22 February\n1823. A first son in March 1744, thirteen months after the wedding, then a daughter in 1745,\nis an ordinary sequence. A first son in 1748, five years after the wedding and three years\nafter his sister, is less ordinary. That leans towards the 1744 this record already holds,\nwithout proving it.\n\nStill unresolved, and the record that would resolve it is his baptism: Grez-Doiceau parish\nregister, 1744 and 1747–48. Not indexed by name in either archive, so it needs the register\nimages read page by page — or FamilySearch's full-text search over them."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"georges2_t","name":"Georges Thumas","dates":"24 Jan 1804 Grez-Doiceau – 12 Jan 1864 Grez-Doiceau","confidence":"doc","surname":"Thumas","family":"thumas","born":"24 Jan 1804 Grez-Doiceau","order":"1804-01-24","died":"12 Jan 1864 Grez-Doiceau","occupation":"menuisier (carpenter)","branch":"Thumas","line":"thumas","father":"georgeslambert_t","mother":"quinart","spouses":[{"id":"joostens","name":"Marie Catharina Joostens","detail":"Grez-Doiceau, 17 Feb 1835"}],"source":"Death act nr. 35 — Marie Catherine Joostens, Grez-Doiceau, 10 June 1857 (read on AGATHA); Kraainem marriage act nr. 2, 20 June 1872 — Thumas × Bossin (read on AGATHA); Marriage act — Georges Carolus Josephus Thumas × Antonia Bossin, Kraainem, 20 June 1872; Geneanet","note":"Third of the four carpenter generations. Civil records give his birth as 24 Jan 1804 (earlier trees said 1805).\n\nCORROBORATED (July 2026) as a father, by his son's Kraainem marriage act of 20 June 1872,\nwhich names the bridegroom's parents as Georges Thumas and Maria Catharina Joostens — this\ncouple. He appears again in his wife's 1857 death act ([[S9]]) as her surviving husband.\n\nACT READ (July 2026): named as the bridegroom's father in the Kraainem act of 20 June 1872,\nwith [[joostens]] as the mother — the same couple his wife's 1857 death act shows.\n\nAGE CONFLICT (July 2026), recorded and left open. His wife's death act of 10 June 1857\nnames him as her surviving husband — *Georges Thumas, menuisier of Grez-Doiceau*, the trade\nthis record already carries, so the identification is not in doubt. But it gives his age as\n**63**, which puts his birth around 1793–94. This record says **24 January 1804**, which\nwould have made him 53.\n\nTen years is too wide to be the usual slippage, and the same act gets his wife's age right —\n47, agreeing with her recorded 1810. So one of the two is wrong and it is not obvious which:\nthe 1804 date has never been documented, and the 63 was spoken by whoever reported the death.\n\nNothing changed. FRONTIER: the Grez-Doiceau baptisms for 1793–94 and for 1804, and his own\n1864 death act, which would state an age to compare. His father [[georgeslambert_t]] was born\nin 1772, so either year makes an ordinary age at fatherhood — the parent link does not decide\nit."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"georges_cj","name":"Georges Carolus Josephus Thumas","dates":"15 Sep 1836 Grez-Doiceau – 26 Oct 1922 Sint-Stevens-Woluwe","confidence":"doc","surname":"Thumas","family":"thumas","born":"15 Sep 1836 Grez-Doiceau","order":"1836-09-15","died":"26 Oct 1922 Sint-Stevens-Woluwe","occupation":"landbouwer & herbergier (farmer & innkeeper)","branch":"Thumas","line":"thumas","father":"georges2_t","mother":"joostens","spouses":[{"id":"bossin","name":"Antoina Bossin","detail":"20 Jun 1872 (Kraainem)"}],"source":"Kraainem marriage act nr. 2, 20 June 1872 — Thumas × Bossin (read on AGATHA); Marriage act — Georges Carolus Josephus Thumas × Antonia Bossin, Kraainem, 20 June 1872; Kraainem marriage act nr. 2, 3 February 1902 — Thumas × Vandenbemden; Geneanet","note":"The migration generation: born in the Thumas heartland of Grez-Doiceau, he broke the four-generation carpentry tradition to become a farmer and innkeeper, moving north to Kraainem and then Sint-Stevens-Woluwe — founding the Kraainem Thumas family. Widowed of Antoina Bossin (d. 1894), he later partnered Maria Dehoux.\n\nDOCUMENTED (July 2026) as the bridegroom's father in the Kraainem marriage act of 3 February 1902 (akte nr. 2, [[S5]]), living at Kraainem, with\nhis trade given as *landbouwer en herbergier* — exactly the occupation this record already\ncarried, which is itself a check that this is the same man. The act fixes him as the\nfather of [[jbgeorgius]] and the husband of [[bossin]].\n\nHis own marriage act — Kraainem, 20 June 1872, naming his parents as Georges Thumas and\nMaria Catharina Joostens — is held in the harvested corpus and not yet read on AGATHA.\n\nALSO DOCUMENTED (July 2026) by his own marriage act — Kraainem, 20 June 1872 — the one\nthis record's `detail` already dated. It names him as the bridegroom, born at Grez-Doiceau,\nson of **Georges Thumas** and **Maria Catharina Joostens**, so it proves the links to\n[[georges2_t]] and [[joostens]] as well as the marriage to [[bossin]].\n\nACT READ (July 2026). His own 1872 marriage act gives his birth as 15 September 1836 at\nGrez-Doiceau — as held — and his trade then as *fabrieksgast*, a factory hand. Thirty years\nlater, in his son's 1902 act, he is a *landbouwer en herbergier*. One man, two stages of a\nlife; the occupation field keeps the later one because that is how the family is remembered,\nand this note keeps the earlier.\n\nFRONTIER — a daughter the tree does not have. Searching AGATHA for Thumas at Kraainem\nreturned a second act beside the 1872 marriage: a **Kraainem marriage of 15 April 1899**,\nin which the bride is **Henrica Thumas** and her parents are given as *Georgius Carolus\nJosephus Thumas* and *Antonia Bossin* — this couple. So Henrica is a daughter of theirs and\na sister of [[jbgeorgius]], and she belongs in the tree under objective 2.\n\nShe is named here rather than grafted: the act has not been opened, so her birth date, her\nhusband and her own line are all unread. Opening it is the obvious next move, and it would\nname her husband's parents too."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"georgeslambert_t","name":"Georges Lambert Thumas","dates":"17 Feb 1772 Grez-Doiceau – 22 Jan 1863 Grez-Doiceau","confidence":"sup","surname":"Thumas","family":"thumas","born":"17 Feb 1772 Grez-Doiceau","order":"1772-02-17","died":"22 Jan 1863 Grez-Doiceau","occupation":"menuisier (carpenter)","branch":"Thumas","line":"thumas","father":"georges1_t","mother":"noel_t","spouses":[{"id":"quinart","name":"Marie Anne Catherine Quinart (Kinart)","detail":"Grez-Doiceau, 9 Feb 1801"}],"source":"Geneanet","note":"Second of the four carpenter generations; had ten children and lived to age 90.\n\nCHECKED AND REJECTED (July 2026) — the corpus's best match for this record is not him.\nIt offers *Lambert Thumas*, who died at **Brussels on 28 February 1801**, and scores it 46\nbits on surname, place of origin (Grez-Doiceau), a mother and a spouse. Ours died at\nGrez-Doiceau in **1863**, sixty-two years later.\n\nWhat produced the score is worth naming, because it will recur: the agreement on \"mother\"\nand \"spouse\" is on FORENAMES only. That man's mother was Marie Joseph **Docq** and his wife\nMarie Catherine **Becquevort**; ours are Marie Catherine **Noël** and Marie Anne Catherine\n**Quinart**. Two women called Marie Catherine in one Walloon village is no evidence at all,\nand the surnames — the part that would have been evidence — disagree outright.\n\nA high score with a dead man's date sixty years out is the shape of a coincidence, not a\nfind. Left ungrafted and uncited."});
