FamilyTree.person({"id":"gustaaf","name":"Gustaaf Dekeyser","dates":"b. 1920–1929 Oostende","confidence":"sup","surname":"Dekeyser","family":"dekeyser","born":"1920–1929 Oostende","order":"1920","branch":"DeKeyser","line":"de-keyser","father":"jerome_dk","mother":"leonie_p","spouses":[{"id":"simonne","name":"Simonne Vandewalle"}],"source":"stefpaelinck (Stef Paelinck); The family itself","note":"Source detail: Family memory (Roland’s father). Documentary lead: = Gustavus-Josephus Dekeyser b.1899 (stefpaelinck tree, Oostende), Jerome’s brother — unconfirmed\n\nCONFIRMED placement: Cosette confirms Jerome is Roland’s GRANDFATHER, so Gustaaf is Jerome & Léonie’s son — a younger brother of Albert (1920–1972), born ~1920–1928 in Oostende. (This rules out the earlier idea that he was Jerome’s brother Gustavus-Josephus b.1899.) He & Simonne were in England during WWII, where Roland was born in 1943 (plus a daughter). UNCERTAIN: Shaun wondered whether Gustaaf may have died during the war (perhaps in Belgium or France) — but he’s not sure, so this is only a possibility to check, NOT established. He simply isn’t in Belgian civil indexes for a Gustave Dekeyser born ~1920s Oostende, which fits either a wartime death abroad OR a life lived mostly outside Belgium. Open leads to chase next (they’d resolve his fate either way): (1) Roland’s 1943 birth certificate — England & Wales GRO, Edmonton registration district, Q3/Q4 1943, surname De Keyser/Dekeyser, mother’s maiden name Vandewalle (via FreeBMD/GRO); (2) a UK marriage of Gustaaf × Simonne, 1940–43; (3) UK death indexes for a Gustave De Keyser; (4) if he served, Free Belgian Forces / Brigade Piron rolls; (5) Belgian war-dead databases (bel-memorial.org; De Plate Oostende heemkring) — only relevant IF he died in the war.\n\nCHECKED, AND THE CORPUS DOES NOT HAVE HIM (July 2026). His best candidates score 27 bits\nand are **Josse De Keyser of Aalst, 1809**, **Petrus De Keyser of Aalst, 1840**, and an\nAlphonse De Keyser at Leuven in 1952. The first two are a century out and in the wrong\nprovince; none has a date or place in common with him.\n\nThe score comes from the surname plus a shared forename among children, which for De Keyser\nin Oost-Vlaanderen is close to no information at all. This is a clean negative: the open\nBelgian corpus as harvested so far holds nothing that could be Gustaaf, which is consistent\nwith what his record already suspects — that his adult life, and possibly his death, happened\noutside Belgium.\n\nThe leads that matter are still the English ones listed above."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"gustavus_j","name":"Gustavus-Josephus Dekeyser","dates":"b. 19 May 1899 Oostende","confidence":"sup","surname":"Dekeyser","family":"dekeyser","sex":"m","born":"19 May 1899 Oostende","order":"1899-05-19","branch":"DeKeyser","line":"de-keyser","father":"edouard_dk","mother":"louise_bocklandt","source":"stefpaelinck (Stef Paelinck)","note":"Source detail: Stef Paelinck (stefpaelinck) tree; Oostende birth 19 May 1899\n\nJerome’s brother (son of Édouard & Louise). Was briefly considered as a candidate for Roland’s father Gustaaf, but the family confirms Jerome — not Édouard — is Roland’s grandfather, so this Gustavus-Josephus is a great-uncle, not the father. No spouse or death recorded in the tree."});
