FamilyTree.person({"id":"rene_dv","name":"René Devriendt","dates":"","confidence":"fam","surname":"Devriendt","family":"devriendt","sex":"m","branch":"Devriendt","line":"devriendt","father":"marcel_d","mother":"yvonne"});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"rene_j","name":"René Janssens","dates":"d. after 2000 Oostende","confidence":"fam","surname":"Janssens","family":"janssens","died":"after 2000 Oostende","branch":"Thumas","line":"janssens","spouses":[{"id":"anna_vc","name":"Anna Van Craenenbroeck"}],"note":"A witloof (Belgian-endive / “wit goud”) farmer — the family grew the famous chicory around Zaventem (Vlaams-Brabant). René and Anna later moved to the coast (Middelkerke), and René died in Oostende (after 2000). His Janssens ancestry is not yet cracked — but NOT because of any hard privacy barrier. Belgium's civil-record publicity rule is graduated (births open at 100 yrs, marriages 75, deaths 50) AND, crucially, a direct descendant may obtain a relative's birth/marriage/death act at any age, so the documents that name René's parents ARE obtainable by the family. The reason online searching hasn't found him is mundane: (a) 'Janssens' is one of Belgium's commonest surnames, so with no anchor (his birth village or year, or a parent's name) his records can't be told apart from thousands of others; and (b) no descendant has published him in an online tree, and his post-2000 coast death notice isn't in the digitised obituary aggregators (inmemoriam, ingedachten, DELA all checked, July 2026). CONTRAST with his wife's side: Anna Van Craenenbroeck's Zaventem family WAS located (see anna_vc) because that surname is rare and village-specific. UNLOCKS for René: his birth village + approx year (enough to request the birth act and pull his parents), a bidprentje, or a relative's memory of his parents' names."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"renee","name":"Renée Bundervoet","dates":"b. 21 Jun 2017 Leuven","confidence":"fam","surname":"Bundervoet","family":"bundervoet","sex":"f","born":"21 Jun 2017 Leuven","order":"2017-06-21","line":"immediate","father":"shaun","mother":"dorien","note":"Born in Leuven, 21 June 2017 — the elder of the two youngest, and Léon's sister. She and Léon are what this tree was built for. The two of them were previously held in a single entry, \"Renée & Léon Bundervoet\"; split apart so each has their own dates."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"rita_dk","name":"Rita De Keyser","dates":"b. 1940–1949","confidence":"fam","surname":"De Keyser","family":"dekeyser","sex":"f","born":"1940–1949","order":"1940","branch":"DeKeyser","line":"de-keyser","father":"gustaaf","mother":"simonne","source":"The family itself","note":"Roland's only sister, born in the early-to-mid 1940s — in England if she was born\nalongside him during the family's wartime years there, in Oostende if before or after.\nHer birth record is one of the two routes that would confirm Gustaaf × Simonne as a\ncouple, the other being Roland's own 1943 certificate.\n\nMERGED (July 2026). This record and a second one, `roland_sis`, described the same\nperson: both were daughters of Gustaaf × Simonne, both were \"Roland's sister\", both\nborn in the 1940s. `roland_sis` was written when the family could recall a sister but\nnot her name, and this one after the name came out; nothing ever distinguished them.\nThe family knows of one sister, and Gustaaf's record likewise says one daughter. The\ntwo are now one, with `roland_sis` deleted rather than left as a second copy.\n\nNAME. \"Rita\" is family recollection, held with some confidence but not documented.\n\nSURNAME UNCERTAIN — and deliberately so. Her brother's 1943 Tottenham registration is\nknown to have mangled the Flemish name, and the family does not know how Rita's own\nwas written down. The spelling here is the family's form, not a transcription of any\nrecord: treat \"De Keyser\" as a placeholder until a document settles it. Variants\nalready seen or plausible for an English registrar: Dekeyser, Dekeyster, De Kayser,\nKeyser. Any index search must run across all of them rather than the Flemish form\nalone — the wrong spelling is the likeliest reason such a search comes back empty.\n\nFRONTIER: England & Wales GRO birth index, Edmonton registration district (which\ncovered Tottenham) and its neighbours, 1940–1949, mother's maiden name Vandewalle.\nThe mother's-maiden-name field is the strong discriminator — it survives whatever the\nregistrar did to the surname, and it would tie Simonne to this family at the same time."});
